You cannot find all 105 items using ffuf alone because many of them are internal backend workers, cron jobs, database triggers, or third-party webhooks that do not sit at public URLs—and most API endpoints reject standard GET requests without the right HTTP method (POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE), headers, or JSON payloads.
However, to discover as many of the HTTP-exposed endpoints from that list as possible in an authorized target, you must combine multi-method fuzzing, API-specific wordlists, and JavaScript/OpenAPI inspection.

1. Build a Targeted Wordlist for Those 105 Workflows

Standard web wordlists (like common.txt) look for static files (/admin, /backup.zip), not modern API routes. Combine SecLists API wordlists with custom route names derived from the 105 items:
bash
# Combine SecLists API routes with custom financial/auth keywords
cat /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/Web-Content/api/api-endpoints.txt \
    /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/Web-Content/api/objects.txt \
    /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/Web-Content/api/actions.txt \
    > custom_105_wordlist.txt
Use code with caution.

2. Fuzz Across Multiple HTTP Methods (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE)

Modern endpoints like /api/v1/withdraw, /api/v1/transfer, or /api/v1/webhook/stripe often return 404 Not Found on a GET request and only reveal themselves (405 Method Not Allowed, 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, or 422 Unprocessable Entity) when hit with POST or PATCH.
Use ffuf in clusterbomb mode to test both the HTTP method and the endpoint path simultaneously:
bash
# Create a file of HTTP methods
printf "GET\nPOST\nPUT\nPATCH\nDELETE\nOPTIONS\n" > methods.txt

# Run ffuf against your authorized lab target
ffuf -w methods.txt:METHOD \
     -w custom_105_wordlist.txt:ENDPOINT \
     -X METHOD \
     -u https://target-lab.local \
     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
     -H "Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TEST_TOKEN" \
     -d '{}' \
     -mc 200,201,204,400,401,403,405,415,422,500 \
     -fc 404 \
     -t 40 -rate 50
Use code with caution.
• Why -mc 400,401,403,405,422,500 matters: A 400 Bad Request or 422 Unprocessable Entity proves the endpoint exists and is actively validating your empty {} JSON body. A 401/403 proves a protected admin or financial route exists.

3. Fuzz API Versions and Nested Resources

Many of the 105 workflows live under versioned prefixes or nested user/account paths:
bash
printf "v1\nv2\nv3\ninternal\nadmin\nwebhook\nwebhooks\nauth\npayments\n" > prefixes.txt

ffuf -w prefixes.txt:PREFIX \
     -w custom_105_wordlist.txt:ENDPOINT \
     -X POST \
     -u https://target-lab.local \
     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
     -d '{}' \
     -mc all -fc 404
Use code with caution.

4. What ffuf Will Miss (And How to Find the Rest)

Because ffuf only guesses URL paths, pair it with these techniques in your authorized lab to uncover the remaining items from the 105 list:

Target Category	Why ffuf Misses It	How to Discover It Instead
GraphQL Mutations (#93)	Everything goes through a single /graphql URL.	Send an Introspection Query to dump all queries, mutations, and input types.
Frontend-Called Routes (#1–30, #51–65)	Complex nested paths (e.g., /api/org/123/billing/prorate).	Extract paths from client-side JavaScript bundles using tools like LinkFinder or katana.
API Specifications	Undocumented parameters and exact HTTP methods.	Fuzz for /swagger.json, /openapi.json, /api-docs, or /v2/api-docs.
Internal Workers & Cron Jobs (#46, #49, #62, #103)	No HTTP listener exists; triggered by queues or timers.	Inspect source code, Dockerfiles, crontab, or message broker configs (RabbitMQ/Kafka/Redis) in white-box reviews.
Third-Party Webhooks (#34, #45, #59, #88)	Protected by unguessable URL tokens or HMAC signatures.	Check integration settings pages in the UI or provider webhook logs.

